-Response-derived states offer inexpensive feedback for stateful protocol fuzzing, but state novelty is an imperfect proxy for code progress. Requests generated by large language models (LLMs) can amplify this mismatch when unproductive proposals receive durable mutation resources. We present LoopFuzz, an evidence-calibrated controller design combining execute-before-promote admission with online state-productivity calibration. Bounded trials separate structural validity, response acceptability, and reachability from code and inferred protocol state machine (IPSM) novelty. Code-supported candidates qualify for durable admission; state-only candidates receive provisional validation and expire without subsequent code evidence. Discounted Beta--Bernoulli updates and Thompson sampling combine estimated code productivity with frontier exploration, without claiming to recover semantic protocol states. A retrospective analysis of 642 experimental records across nine targets provides descriptive coverage, cost, and mechanism measurements. Observed code-branch and IPSM state-edge endpoints do not consistently reproduce the hypothesized mismatch across motivating targets. Four vulnerability case studies add replay evidence; controlled rediscovery benefits remain unmeasured. A five-arm evaluation protocol specifies matched model configurations, intervention policies, and resource ceilings to isolate admission and calibration. Heterogeneous observation horizons and incomplete lifecycle evidence restrict current findings to descriptive results.
